<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
        <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All About the Weather</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Dress for the Weather.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Identify simple story elements (e.g., characters, setting). Reading: Find specific information in a text (e.g., names, quantities, dates). Phonemic Awareness: Identify a spoken word's initial and final phoneme if these words have been previously encountered and are attached to a picture.</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify simple story elements, such as characters or settings, in short texts about the weather (e.g., recognizing "the boy" and "the rainy day" in a story). Can find specific weather-related information in a text (e.g., locating "rain" or "sunny" when looking for weather descriptions or identifying the number of items, such as "two umbrellas"). Can identify the initial and final phonemes of spoken weather-related words and make the correspondence between letters and their sounds at the beginning and end of words with visual support (e.g., identifying the /w/ and /i/ sounds in "windy" when shown an image of wind).</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows large flashcards of different weather conditions (sunny, rainy, windy) and corresponding clothing items (hat, umbrella, coat, boots, scarf). Students identify the pictures as the teacher asks, "What is the weather like?" and "What do I wear?" The teacher points and says, "It's a sunny day. I like sunny days. I wear a hat."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words like "sunny," "rainy," and "windy" using TPR. For "sunny," the teacher smiles and makes a 'sun' gesture. For "rainy," the teacher wiggles fingers down like rain. The teacher emphasizes the /s/ sound in "sunny" and the /r/ sound in "rainy." The teacher also models phrases: "It's a sunny day," "It's a rainy day," "I wear a coat." For "windy," the teacher shows a picture of wind and slowly pronounces "windy," clearly articulating the initial /w/ sound and the final /i/ sound, asking students to repeat. </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses visuals to check understanding.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of rain and asks: "Is this a sunny day or a rainy day?" (Expected answer: "A rainy day.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of a coat and asks: "Do I wear this (points to coat) on a hot day or a cold day?" (Expected answer: "A cold day.")</li>
                    </ul></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All About the Weather."</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a very short, illustrated narrative text (a simple story) titled "What Should Tom Wear Today?" projected on the board or as individual handouts with large pictures. The story features a boy named Tom and describes two simple weather conditions and what Tom wears.
                    <br><i>Example Text:</i><br>
                    "Today is a sunny day! Tom likes sunny days. He wears a hat and plays outside.
                    Now it is a rainy day. Tom needs his umbrella and a yellow coat. He wears boots. He plays inside."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding short, simple written texts on familiar topics.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> The teacher reads the story aloud while pointing to the words and pictures. Then, the teacher asks: "Who is in the story?" (Expected answer: "Tom.") "What is the story about?" (Expected answer: "Weather and clothes.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher reads simple statements, and students show a "thumbs up" for True or "thumbs down" for False.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Tom likes rainy days." (False)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Tom wears a hat on a sunny day." (True)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher provides two large pictures (one sunny, one rainy). Students point to the picture that matches the sentence the teacher reads from the story, e.g., "He needs his umbrella." (Students point to the rainy picture.)</li>
                    </ul></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Identifying simple story elements (characters, setting). Reading: Finding specific information in a text. Phonemic Awareness: Initial and final phonemes /w/ and /i/ in "windy". Grammar: Adjectives + nouns (e.g., "rainy day"), Present simple (e.g., "He wears"), WH-questions ("What is the weather like?"). Vocabulary: sun, rain, coat, hat, day, cloud, wind, umbrella, boots, scarf, sunny, rainy, windy.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Reading & Matching):</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with short sentences or phrases related to the story and pictures. Students draw a line to match the sentence to the correct picture.
                            <br><i>Examples:</i> "a sunny day" → picture of sun; "wears a coat" → picture of boy in a coat; "Tom" → picture of Tom.
                            The teacher specifically includes a picture of "windy" with the word "windy" below it. Students circle the letter 'W' for the beginning sound and 'Y' for the end sound in "windy" on their worksheet, guided by the teacher.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Reading & Circling):</b> The teacher provides sentences with two choices in parentheses based on the story. Students read the sentence and circle the correct word.
                            <br><i>Examples:</i>
                            "It's a (sunny / rainy) day. Tom wears a hat."
                            "He needs an (umbrella / hat) for the rain."
                            "Tom plays (inside / outside) when it is rainy."
                            The teacher ensures these sentences reinforce finding specific information from the story.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Check (Reading Aloud & Pointing):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of picture cards (weather and clothing) and simple sentence strips. Student A reads a sentence strip (e.g., "It is a sunny day. I wear a hat."). Student B points to the correct weather picture and clothing item. Then they switch roles. The teacher circulates, checking for accurate reading and correct identification. This activity also checks comprehension of "What is the weather like?" and "I wear..." expressions.</li>
                    </ul></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Dress the Paper Doll for the Weather" (Collaborative Reading & Drawing/Cutting).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive a paper doll cutout and a sheet with various clothing items (hat, coat, umbrella, boots, scarf, jacket) and weather icons (sun, rain, cloud, wind) to cut out.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher places different "Weather Cards" around the room, each with a simple sentence describing the weather and suggesting an activity or clothing. (e.g., Card 1: "It's a sunny day. Wear a hat." Card 2: "It's a rainy day. Wear boots and take an umbrella." Card 3: "It's a windy day. Wear a jacket.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In small groups, students read one weather card. They must find and cut out the appropriate weather icon and clothing items mentioned on the card.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They paste the weather icon next to their paper doll and "dress" the doll with the correct clothes according to what they read.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students then present their paper doll to another group, briefly pointing to the weather icon and clothing items. E.g., "It's a sunny day. He wears a hat." or "It's a rainy day, and she wears a coat and boots." This encourages the use of adjectives + nouns and present simple.</li>
                    </ul></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Dress the Paper Doll" activity and a short individual worksheet.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a worksheet with three short sentences describing weather and an empty space next to each. Students read each sentence and draw the correct clothing item or weather condition.
                    <br><i>Examples:</i>
                    1. "It is sunny. I like sunny days!" (Students draw a sun.)
                    2. "It is rainy. I need my umbrella." (Students draw an umbrella.)
                    3. "It is windy. I wear a jacket." (Students draw a windy scene or a jacket.)</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation & Worksheet):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identifies Story Elements:</b> Correctly draws sun/rain (setting).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Finds Specific Information:</b> Draws correct clothing (umbrella, jacket) based on the text.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Uses Target Vocabulary:</b> Demonstrates understanding of "sunny," "rainy," "windy," "umbrella," "jacket."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> (Informal observation) Ability to recognize "windy" (e.g., when teacher prompts during task).</li>
                    </ul></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick "Exit Ticket" by circling a smiley face, a neutral face, or a sad face for each statement:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read about sunny days."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can find 'umbrella' in a story."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I know the sound for 'w' in windy."</li>
                    </ul></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was your favorite part about reading today's story?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new weather words or clothing words did you understand better?</li>
                    </ul></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <br>What aspects of reading comprehension (identifying story elements, finding specific information) worked well with the 2nd graders? Were the texts and tasks appropriately challenging for Pre-A1.4? The visual support was crucial for their understanding of "rainy day" and "sunny day." The phonemic awareness activity for /w/ and /i/ in "windy" needs continued reinforcement with more examples. I noticed some students still struggled with distinguishing "coat" and "jacket" when reading, requiring additional visual cues. I should ensure more varied short texts are used in future reading lessons. What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to reinforce these reading skills in a speaking context?</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on the vocabulary and comprehension from today's reading lesson. Students will verbally describe the weather and what they wear, using phrases like "It's a [weather] day, and I wear a [clothing item]," actively engaging in discussions about preferences, such as "I like sunny days."</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>